    "author_association": "MEMBER",
    "body": "### Problem\n\nDot-repeating the operator that acted on the visual selection from custom mapping can result into the `E1255: <Cmd> mapping must end with <CR>` error. Although the mapping does end with `<CR>`.\n\nThis starts to happen after 64a301184ef3 (#41297). cc @justinmk\n\n### Steps to reproduce\n\n1. `nvim --clean`\n2. `:xmap <M-m> <Cmd>normal! $<CR>`\n3. Create two lines in the current buffer: `i`-`aaa`-`<CR>`-`bbb`-`<Esc>`.\n4. Put cursor on the first line and type `v`-`<M-m>`-`d`. It deletes to the end of line as expected.\n5. Put cursor on the other line and press `.`. The `E1255: <Cmd> mapping must end with <CR>` error appears while the visual selection+mode are activated.\n\nNotes:\n- Doing `v`-`<M-m>`-`c` (instead of `d` operator) does not throw an error, but it doesn't do the dot-repeat either (the visual selection+mode are activated).\n\n### Expected behavior\n\nThe dot-repeat works without throwing an error.\n\n### Nvim version (nvim -v)\n\nNVIM v0.13.0-dev-1321+gb169d9376c\n\n### Vim (not Nvim) behaves the same?\n\nNo\n\n### Operating system/version\n\nEndeavourOS Linux x86_64, 7.1.6-arch1-1\n\n### Terminal name/version\n\nGhostty 1.3.1-arch2\n\n### $TERM environment variable\n\nxterm-ghostty\n\n### Installation\n\nAppimage and from source",
    "closed_at": null,
